# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the config-linux-llvm-tsan leg
# (CMakePresets.json's test-linux-llvm-tsan preset points TSAN_OPTIONS at this
# file; .github/workflows/_build.yml runs it).
#
# Deliberately near-empty, and to be kept that way. A suppression here hides a
# real report, so each entry must name the library or symbol it silences and
# say why that report is not this project's bug - the same discipline
# .clang-tidy's NOLINT comments and cmake/Sanitizers.cmake's deliberate
# omission of -fsanitize=integer already follow.
#
# The first run of this leg was clean: clang 21, `ctest -L concurrency`, 36
# cases over tests/audio/ and tests/cli/test_cli_live.cpp, no reports. So
# nothing below is suppressing anything today. The one entry that exists is
# for third-party runtime code this project does not own and cannot fix, kept
# as a documented landing spot rather than added reactively under time
# pressure the first time a leg goes red.
#
# Format: one rule per line, <type>:<pattern> - see
# https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/ThreadSanitizerSuppressions
#
# NOT the place for a race in src/ or apps/. If this leg reports one of those,
# the answer is a fix or a documented redesign, not a line here.

# ALSA's own library state. libasound2 initialises global configuration lazily
# and is not built with TSan instrumentation, so a report inside it is
# uninstrumented-library noise rather than a finding about how this project
# calls it. Only reached at all when a CI machine has a sound card - the
# headless containers this leg runs on enumerate zero devices - which is
# exactly the case that would otherwise turn the leg red on one runner and
# green on another.
called_from_lib:libasound.so
